### A · Defining gender-based violence

1. **Gender-based violence** is violence directed at a person because of their **sex or gender** (MCQ 2025).
2. The UN **Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women (DEVAW)** was adopted on ==20 December 1993==.
3. DEVAW Article 1 covers **physical, sexual and psychological** harm, including **threats, coercion** and **deprivation of liberty**, in public or private life.
4. DEVAW Article 2 names three sites: the **family**, the **community** and the **State**.
5. The **Vienna World Conference on Human Rights** (==1993==) declared women's rights to be human rights.
6. CEDAW **General Recommendation 19** (==1992==) declared gender-based violence a form of **discrimination**.
7. CEDAW **General Recommendation 35** (==2017==) updated GR 19.
8. The **Beijing Platform** (==1995==) made violence against women **Critical Area D**.
9. **25 November** is the International Day for the Elimination of Violence against Women (designated ==1999==).
10. The **16 Days of Activism** run from 25 November to **10 December**, Human Rights Day.
11. The Council of Europe's **Istanbul Convention** on violence against women was adopted in ==2011==.
12. **SDG target 5.2** calls for eliminating all forms of violence against women and girls.
13. **SDG target 5.3** calls for eliminating **child, early and forced marriage**.
14. About **1 in 3** women worldwide experience physical or sexual violence (WHO ==2021==; MCQ 2024).
15. About **85,000** women and girls were killed intentionally in ==2023== (UNODC and UN Women).
16. About **51,100** of them were killed by **partners or family members** — one every **10 minutes**.
17. **Liz Kelly** described violence against women as a **continuum** (==1988==).
18. The WHO calls violence against women a public health problem of **epidemic** proportions.
19. **Economic abuse** includes denying a woman food, clothing, shelter or her own income.
20. **Femicide** is the intentional killing of women and girls because they are women.

### B · Theories of violence

21. **Albert Bandura**'s **Bobo doll** experiments (==1961==) showed that children copy aggression.
22. Bandura's *Aggression: A Social Learning Analysis* appeared in ==1973==.
23. According to **social learning theory**, violence is **learned** (MCQ 2023) and **socially constructed** (MCQ 2024).
24. The passing of violence from parents to children is called **intergenerational transmission**.
25. **William Goode** (==1971==) proposed the **resource theory** of family violence.
26. **R. Emerson Dobash and Russell Dobash** wrote *Violence Against Wives* (==1979==).
27. Feminist theory sees **patriarchy** as the root cause of violence against women.
28. **Susan Brownmiller** wrote *Against Our Will* (==1975==), arguing that rape is about **power**.
29. **Lenore Walker** described the **cycle of violence** in *The Battered Woman* (==1979==).
30. Walker's cycle has phases of **tension building, acute battering and loving contrition**.
31. Walker also described "**learned helplessness**" among battered women.
32. The **Power and Control Wheel** was developed by the **Domestic Abuse Intervention Project** in **Duluth**, Minnesota (from ==1981==).
33. **Ellen Pence** and **Michael Paymar** were leading figures of the Duluth model.
34. The hub of the Duluth wheel is **power and control**; its rim is **physical and sexual violence**.
35. **Evan Stark** wrote *Coercive Control* (==2007==).
36. **Michael Johnson** distinguished **intimate terrorism** from **situational couple violence**.
37. **Lori Heise** proposed the **ecological model** of violence against women (==1998==).
38. Heise's four levels are **individual, relationship, community and society**.
39. **Johan Galtung** defined **structural violence** in ==1969==.
40. Galtung added **cultural violence** in ==1990==.
41. **Direct violence** has an identifiable perpetrator; **structural violence** is built into social structures.
42. **Kimberlé Crenshaw**'s "Mapping the Margins" (==1991==) applied intersectionality to violence against women.

### C · Masculinities

43. **R. W. Connell** introduced **hegemonic masculinity** (*Gender and Power* ==1987==; *Masculinities* ==1995==).
44. The **patriarchal dividend** is the advantage men gain from women's subordination (Connell).
45. Connell's hierarchy: **hegemonic, complicit, marginalised and subordinated** masculinities.
46. **Michael Kaufman** described the **triad of men's violence** (==1987==).
47. Kaufman's triad: violence against **women**, against **other men**, and against **themselves**.
48. **Michael Kimmel** described "**aggrieved entitlement**".
49. **Rozan**, an Islamabad NGO founded in ==1998==, works with boys, men and police on gender and violence.
50. The **White Ribbon Campaign** of men against violence began in Canada in ==1991==.
51. UN Women launched the **HeForShe** campaign in ==2014==.
52. *Ghairat* and *izzat* are the Pakistani concepts of male **honour** linked to women's behaviour.

### D · Forms and data in Pakistan

53. **28%** of ever-married women in Pakistan have experienced physical violence since age 15 (**PDHS 2017–18**).
54. **34%** of ever-married women have experienced spousal physical, sexual or emotional violence (PDHS 2017–18).
55. **56%** of women who suffered violence never sought help (PDHS 2017–18).
56. **SSDO** recorded **32,617** gender-based violence cases in ==2024==.
57. SSDO recorded **5,339** rape cases in 2024.
58. SSDO recorded **547** honour killings in 2024.
59. Conviction rates in 2024 were about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing (SSDO).
60. **HRCP** recorded at least **405** honour killings in ==2024==.
61. *Karo-kari* is the Sindhi term for honour killing.
62. *Siyahkari* is the Balochi term; *tor tora* the Pashto term; *kala kali* the Punjabi term.
63. ***Vani*** or ***swara*** is giving girls in marriage to settle a dispute.
64. ***Watta satta*** is exchange marriage.
65. **Stove burning** is the killing or burning of women disguised as kitchen accidents.
66. Six-year-old **Zainab Ansari** was raped and murdered in **Kasur** in ==2018==.
67. The **motorway gang rape** near Lahore took place in ==September 2020==.
68. **Noor Mukadam** was murdered in Islamabad in ==2021==; the Supreme Court upheld her killer's death sentence in ==2025==.
69. **Sana Yousaf**, a 17-year-old social-media creator, was shot dead in Islamabad in ==June 2025==.
70. A couple were killed on a tribal chief's orders in **Degari**, Balochistan, in ==2025==.
71. **Samia Sarwar** was killed in her lawyers' Lahore office in ==1999==.
72. **Qandeel Baloch** was killed by her brother in **Multan** in ==July 2016==.
73. Qandeel Baloch's brother was acquitted by the Lahore High Court in ==2022== after a parental pardon.

### E · Laws

74. The **Zina Ordinance** of ==1979== endangered rape victims with adultery charges.
75. The **Qisas and Diyat Ordinance** (==1990==) let victims' heirs **forgive** killers.
76. The **Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2004** defined honour crimes.
77. The **Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act 2006** moved rape back into the **Penal Code**.
78. The **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act** was passed in ==2010==.
79. The workplace harassment law was amended in ==2022== to cover more workers.
80. **Section 509 PPC** punishes harassment in public places (amended ==2010==).
81. The **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act** was passed in ==2011==.
82. The Acid Act added **sections 336A and 336B** to the Penal Code.
83. Acid attacks carry **14 years to life** imprisonment and a minimum fine of **one million rupees**.
84. The **Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act** was passed in ==2011==.
85. Section **310A PPC** punishes giving a woman in *vani* or *swara*.
86. Section **498B PPC** punishes forced marriage; **498C** marriage to the Qur'an; **498A** denial of inheritance.
87. **Sindh** passed the first provincial domestic-violence law in ==2013==.
88. **Balochistan** passed its domestic-violence law in ==2014==.
89. **Khyber Pakhtunkhwa** passed its domestic-violence law in ==2021==.
90. **Islamabad**'s Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act was passed in ==2025==.
91. The **Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2016** restricted the family-forgiveness loophole in honour killings (MCQ 2026).
92. The 2016 honour law requires **life imprisonment** even if the family forgives.
93. Senator **Sughra Imam** moved the anti-honour-killing bill in ==2014==.
94. The **2016** anti-rape amendment required **DNA** testing.
95. The **Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act (PECA)** was passed in ==2016==.
96. The **Zainab Alert, Response and Recovery Act** was passed in ==2020==.
97. The **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act** received assent on ==1 December 2021==.
98. The 2021 Act created **Special Sexual Offences Investigation Units** and **Anti-Rape Crisis Cells**.
99. The **Prevention of Trafficking in Persons Act** was passed in ==2018==.

### F · The Punjab Act 2016 and institutions

100. The **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act** was passed on ==24 February 2016==.
101. Its section 2(r) lists **domestic violence, sexual violence, psychological abuse, economic abuse, stalking and cybercrime**.
102. Complaints under the Punjab Act go to the **Family Court**, which must decide within **90 days**.
103. The Act provides **interim, protection, residence and monetary** orders.
104. A protection order may require a **GPS** ankle or wrist bracelet for grave violence.
105. Breach of an order is punishable by up to **one year**; repeat breach by up to **two years**.
106. A false complaint is punishable by up to **three months** or a fine.
107. The Act created **District Women Protection Committees** and **Women Protection Officers**.
108. The first **Violence against Women Centre** opened in **Multan** in ==2017==.
109. The **Punjab Women Helpline** number is **1043**.
110. The first **women's police stations** opened in ==1994==.
111. The **National Commission on the Status of Women** was set up in ==2000== (Act ==2012==).
112. ***Dar-ul-Aman*** are government shelters for women.

### G · The case studies

113. **Mukhtaran Mai** was gang-raped on a *panchayat*'s orders in **Meerwala**, Muzaffargarh, on ==22 June 2002==.
114. Mukhtaran Mai belonged to **South Punjab** (MCQ 2016).
115. The Supreme Court upheld the acquittal of five accused in the Mukhtaran Mai case on ==21 April 2011==.
116. Mukhtaran Mai's memoir is ***In the Name of Honor*** (==2006==).
117. **Malala Yousafzai** was born on ==12 July 1997== in **Mingora**, Swat (MCQ 2016).
118. Malala wrote a **BBC Urdu** diary as "**Gul Makai**" in ==2009==.
119. Malala was shot by the Taliban on ==9 October 2012==.
120. Malala addressed the **United Nations** on ==12 July 2013== — "Malala Day".
121. Malala shared the **Nobel Peace Prize** with **Kailash Satyarthi** in ==2014==, at **17**.
122. Malala works internationally for girls' **education** (MCQ 2019).
123. **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy** is a **filmmaker** (MCQ 2025), born in Karachi in ==1978==.
124. ***Saving Face*** (Oscar ==2012==) is about **acid attacks**; co-directed with **Daniel Junge**.
125. ***A Girl in the River*** (Oscar ==2016==) is about **honour killing** and **Saba Qaiser** (MCQ 2019).
